# How to use NixOS / Nix package manager


>> Basics

> OS github
  * https://github.com/nixos/nixpkgs

> Configurations
  * Configuration file: /etc/nixos/configuration.nix
      ! there's also "hardware-configuration.nix", though you'd rarely want to modify it
    ``` sudo nixos-rebuild switch  # rebuild system and switch to new version (doesn't update packages)
    ``` sudo nixos-rebuild test  # build and activate version, but don't add it to the bootloader
    ``` sudo nix-collect-garbage --delete-older-than 14d  # delete versions older than 14 days and packages that aren't used by versions
  * Using Flakes
    ``` sudo nix flake init --template github:vimjoyer/flake-starter-config  # run in /etc/nixos to initialize flake.nix with vimjoyer's template
    ``` nix flake update  # update packages ("flake.lock" file gets updated)
  * NixOS creates new versions as you change your configurations, so you can go back
      * NixOS saves all its packages in nixstore ( /nix/store ) which get referenced by the versions, so they're not duplicated (no waste of space)
  


>> General guidelines

  * Use Flakes, from the start. They'll make your system reproducible (by listing which NixOS repo branch is used, and following package versions with .lock file)
  * Add home-manager ( github:nix-community/home-manager ) to inputs, so you'd have programs per-user (configs saved for user, not globally)
  * You should use Wayland, as X11 is on its way out



